course: dictionary
lesson: 1417
title: Gentle touches
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe how something is touched. Caress is one of them. To caress someone is to touch or stroke them gently and lovingly. Look at the words around it for clues: «The mother caressed her baby's head» tells you the touch was soft and loving, not rough or quick.
explain: Caress can also be a noun, meaning a gentle, loving touch. Poets use the word for things that are not people. In a story, «Poets say the breeze caresses the face» means the wind touches the face softly, like a gentle hand.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If stroked gently still makes sense in place of caressed, you have the right meaning. Hitting or pushing would not fit, so caress is about softness, not force.
fact: Caress means to touch or stroke someone or something gently and lovingly.
fact: As a noun, a caress is a gentle, loving touch.
fact: In «a fine powder filled the air and caressed the cheek with a tingle», the snow touches the face softly, so caressed means touched gently.
fact: Wind or water can caress something too. In The Wind in the Willows, the river seems to caress the boat and the bank.
fact: Caress shows tenderness, so it is a word about kindness and care.
exercise: «The mother caressed her baby's head as it fell asleep.» What does caressed mean? | Shaken awake | *Touched gently and lovingly | Tickled loudly | Pushed hard away
exercise: «Poets say the breeze caresses the face.» What is the breeze doing? | *Touching the face softly | Blowing the face hard | Hiding from the face
exercise: Which sentence uses caress as a noun, meaning a gentle, loving touch? | The puppy caressed her hand. | *The puppy gave her a soft caress. | The puppy caresses the ball, then runs off.
test: «The river seemed to caress the boat and the bank.» What does caress mean here? | Dried up | Crashed hard into | *Touched softly, as water does | Froze solid
test: Which word means the same kind of soft, loving touch as caress? | Slap | *Stroke | Punch | Grab
exam: «a fine powder ... caressed the cheek with a tingle» Why is caressed a good word for snow on a cheek? | *Because the touch is light and gentle, not rough | Because the snow was angry | Because the snow was hitting the cheek hard | Because the cheek was frozen solid
